From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000026 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Port Bot <port@local>
Date: Thu, 18 Sep 2026 10:45:00 +0200
Subject: 0026: don't stall every watch while API discovery re-runs

`useK8sWatchResources` gates its whole `reduxIDs` map on `useModelsLoaded()`,
which reports true only once a discovery sweep is *not* in flight and
latches per component instance. A page mounted while API discovery is
re-running therefore dispatches no watch at all - not even the initial
GET - until that sweep finishes, and shows a loading state the whole
time.

That is the 30s a pod details page can sit on its skeleton. Discovery
re-runs on every CRD add/delete (`startAPIDiscovery`'s CRD watch calls
`getResources()` again) and fans out one request per API group-version
at once, so on a cluster with many CRDs a sweep is not quick - while the
models it needs are already in the store, because `GetResourcesInFlight`
only raises the flag and never clears `RESOURCES.models`. Core's own tabs
still render during such a sweep for exactly that reason; only the
watched object waits.

Dispatch a watch as soon as the model for that resource is known, which
is what the singular `useK8sWatchResource` already does - it uses
`modelsLoaded` solely to decide whether a missing model is an error yet,
and dispatches off `k8sModel` alone. Keep that meaning here: while
discovery is in flight a model that isn't known may simply not have
arrived yet, so its entry is left out of the map and the consumer keeps
its loading state, rather than being reported as a `NoModelError`. Once
discovery settles an unknown model is an error again, unchanged.

Leaving an entry out means `results` can now be asked for a key the map
has no entry for, where before `reduxIDs` was null wholesale and the
optional chain short-circuited - so `reduxIDs?.[key].noModel` becomes
`reduxIDs?.[key]?.noModel` (and likewise for `.id`). That also covers a
pre-existing hole: `getIDAndDispatch` can return falsy, which already
left a key unset and would have thrown.

The first new test fails without this change; the second guards the
behaviour that is deliberately kept.
